WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.033
Why testing AI breaks the old rules.

2
00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:07.798
A test that passes today can fail tomorrow, and nobody touched the code.

3
00:00:07.798 --> 00:00:08.667
Let's see why.

4
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:13.647
Classic unit tests assume the function is deterministic.

5
00:00:13.647 --> 00:00:15.267
Same input, same output.

6
00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:16.799
An LLM breaks that.

7
00:00:16.799 --> 00:00:20.300
Many outputs are valid, and there is no single oracle.

8
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.233
So we replace is it equal with a threshold.

9
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:26.500
Is it correct enough, consistent enough, and safe enough?

10
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.300
There are four reasons for this.

11
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.932
First, there is no single ground truth.

12
00:00:31.932 --> 00:00:35.100
We grade with rubrics, reference answers, and judges.

13
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.241
Second, randomness is real.

14
00:00:37.241 --> 00:00:42.833
Sampling temperature and even floating point order on a GPU can change the output.

15
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.533
Third, the system moves without you.

16
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:51.200
A vendor can swap the model behind an API, so pin versions and rerun your golden set.

17
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.895
And fourth, quality is a distribution.

18
00:00:53.895 --> 00:00:56.100
One passing example proves nothing.

19
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.900
Here is the difference in code.

20
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.800
The exact match fails about thirty percent of runs, even when the summary is good.

21
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:08.233
The robust version checks similarity to a reference against a threshold.

22
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:11.467
Then a judge grades the answer against a rubric.

23
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:13.876
Here is a real case.

24
00:01:13.876 --> 00:01:19.500
The Orbital Research Agent used to write that a close approach will occur soon, with no date.

25
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.133
Exact match can't test the fix, because every report has different numbers.

26
00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:28.533
So the test is a rubric: a date, units, and no vague time words.

27
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:32.333
A judge scores ten reports, rerun on every prompt change.

28
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:39.100
The common mistake: asserting exact equality on free text, then blaming the test runner.

29
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.633
The fix: pin temperature to zero where you must assert exactly, and still say usually.

30
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:46.699
Let's recap.

31
00:01:46.699 --> 00:01:51.100
An LLM output is not deterministic, so equality checks break.

32
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.633
Test with thresholds for correct, consistent, and safe.

33
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:57.767
Pin model versions, and rerun your golden set.

34
00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:00.632
Quality is a distribution, not a point.

35
00:02:00.632 --> 00:02:02.933
Next up: data and pipeline testing.
